Source: venu-pacific
Section: misc
Priority: optional
Maintainer: sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               gettext,
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://venupacific.org
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: venu-pacific-archive-keyring
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: Venu Pacific APT archive keyring and source list
 Installs the OpenPGP public key that signs the Venu Pacific package archive,
 plus the deb822 source entry that points apt at it.
 .
 This is the package that makes "apt update" see Venu Pacific's own releases.
 Without it an installed system only ever talks to the Debian archive, and
 updates to the apps, branding and emergency content below never arrive.

Package: venu-pacific-branding
Architecture: all
Depends: desktop-base,
         plymouth,
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: Venu Pacific visual identity
 Wallpapers, the Plymouth boot theme, the application/login pixmap and the
 Calamares installer branding for Venu Pacific.
 .
 Registers the default desktop background through update-alternatives and
 selects the Plymouth theme, so the look follows the package rather than
 being baked into the image.
 .
 plymouth and desktop-base are hard dependencies rather than the Recommends
 they might look like, because dpkg only guarantees configuration order for
 Depends. As Recommends they were configured AFTER this package in a single
 apt transaction, and the postinst's plymouth-set-default-theme call against
 a half-configured plymouth left plymouth itself stuck at "unpacked" -- which
 is exactly what a fresh image build is.

Package: venu-pacific-content
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: Offline disaster-readiness and services reference data
 The JSON data behind the Disaster Readiness and Services Directory apps:
 cyclone, tsunami and earthquake guidance, and the government, health and
 education contact directory, for Vanuatu and Fiji.
 .
 Split into its own package deliberately. It is the part most likely to need
 correcting between releases and the part where being wrong matters most, and
 at a few hundred kilobytes it can be updated over a metered connection that
 could never carry a new image.

Package: venu-pacific-l10n
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: Translations for Venu Pacific
 Compiled gettext catalogs for the Venu Pacific applications. Currently
 Bislama (bi) and Fijian (fj); English needs no catalog.

Package: venu-pacific-apps
Architecture: all
Depends: gir1.2-gtk-3.0,
         python3,
         python3-gi,
         venu-pacific-content (= ${binary:Version}),
         zenity,
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: venu-pacific-l10n (= ${binary:Version}),
Suggests: kiwix,
Description: Venu Pacific desktop applications
 The GTK applications that make up the Venu Pacific desktop:
 .
  * Welcome - first-run language picker and setup
  * Hub - the launcher that ties the others together
  * Disaster Readiness - offline cyclone/tsunami/earthquake guidance
  * Services Directory - offline government/health/education contacts
  * Help & Feedback, and the offline-content download guide

Package: venu-pacific-assistant
Architecture: all
Depends: cups-client,
         gir1.2-gtk-3.0,
         gir1.2-wnck-3.0,
         poppler-utils,
         python3,
         python3-bs4,
         python3-gi,
         python3-pygments,
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: kiwix-tools,
            zim-tools,
Description: Venu Pacific offline AI assistant
 A local, offline assistant that answers questions against the bundled
 offline encyclopedias and documents on disk. Talks to the llama.cpp server
 shipped in the image at /opt/venu-pacific/llama.cpp.
 .
 Separate from venu-pacific-apps because of its dependency weight: it pulls
 in the window-manager, PDF and ZIM-search tooling the other apps do not
 need, and it is the one component worth removing on the lowest-end hardware.
 .
 python3-pygments provides syntax colouring for code the assistant shows.
 It is a hard dependency rather than optional because a coding answer
 rendered as flat grey text is the failure this package exists to avoid;
 there is a reduced fallback if the import fails, but nothing should be
 relying on it.

Package: venu-pacific-settings
Architecture: all
Depends: light-locker,
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: libnotify-bin,
            synaptic,
Description: Venu Pacific system defaults and update notifier
 Default XFCE settings for new accounts, the login/console branding, the
 screen-locker guard, and the two-part update notification system: a daily
 off-peak root timer that refreshes apt's index and records the upgradable
 count, and a per-user timer that turns that count into a desktop
 notification.
 .
 Neither half ever fetches or installs a package. Bandwidth here is often
 capped or metered, so applying updates stays a deliberate choice made in
 Synaptic.
 .
 Depends on light-locker because it takes over that package's autostart
 entry through a dpkg diversion, substituting a guard that decides whether
 locking is appropriate on this hardware. Diverting a file belonging to a
 package that may not be installed would leave the system with no screen
 locker autostart at all.

Package: venu-pacific-desktop
Architecture: all
Depends: venu-pacific-apps (= ${binary:Version}),
         venu-pacific-archive-keyring (= ${binary:Version}),
         venu-pacific-branding (= ${binary:Version}),
         venu-pacific-content (= ${binary:Version}),
         venu-pacific-l10n (= ${binary:Version}),
         venu-pacific-settings (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: venu-pacific-assistant (= ${binary:Version}),
Description: Venu Pacific desktop (metapackage)
 Depends on everything that makes an installed system a Venu Pacific system.
 Installing this package pulls the whole set; upgrading it carries the whole
 set forward.
 .
 This package also carries the release version: /etc/venu-pacific-release and
 the VENU_PACIFIC_VERSION field in /etc/os-release are derived from it, so
 there is exactly one place a release number is defined - debian/changelog.
